WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.500
One of my co-workers went to a conference in Greece,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.080
and came back with a present,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.520
of something which is very old,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.060
from 400 years B.C. or even earlier,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.800
but I'd never seen before.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.860
It's called the Pythagoras Cup

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.820
and it has this strange bit sticking up in the middle.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.180
And the story is

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.100
that this cup was invented by Pythagoras.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.060
The one that worked on right-angled triangles.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.240
He invented it so when he gave his students wine,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.500
that they all got the same amount

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.260
and if somebody was greedy,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.340
that student

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.120
got his comeuppance

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.060
It behaves normally,

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.220
until you get to this gold ring here, the one I am running my finger around,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.720
not the one one up here.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.300
And if you fill it above that,

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.460
that's somewhere between these two,

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.860
suddenly,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.180
all the liquid in the glass

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.440
drains out

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.060
and through a hole in the bottom.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.980
Of course, you can't see what's happening here, so I rushed off

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.820
and bought a glass one

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.660
so you can see what's happening.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.460
I asked Neil to demonstrate it

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.040
You pour in the water,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.720
imagine it's wine, but if it was wine you couldn't see inside.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.580
And, as the level of wine rises,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.880
suddenly, if you were too greedy,

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.300
it all dribbles out.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:27.300
"sound of water draining"

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:35.060
And, it siphons out through the little tube in the centre.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.160
The way it works

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.320
is that inside here there is a u-tube

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.660
which has a small opening at the bottom.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.120
And as the liquid rises,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.880
the u-tube fills up

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.720
and when it gets to the top,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.380
it siphons 'round

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.940
and sucks the water out.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.100
I was really excited about this,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.520
and then when I was driving home,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.840
I wondered, what would happen if you filled it with mercury.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.140
Mercury is a very dense liquid.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.520
So I persuaded Neil to fill it with Mercury,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.000
and it works just as well with Mercury.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:42.420
And,

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.520
what is interesting

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.160
is that when you do it with water,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.780
the water on our glass

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.000
Pythagoras Cup

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.520
dribbles out at a sort of angle

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.220
because water so-called 'wets' glass; it sticks to the glass.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.460
But with mercury, because it is denser,

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.380
and it doesn't wet glass, it goes straight down.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.260
And makes quite a sort of spectacular

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.440
pattern in the bottom of the dish.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.480
I then had a slightly strange idea:

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.660
I wondered what would have happened if you put just a little mercury in

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:18.740
and then filled the rest with water.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:25.240
And I predicted, because water is much less dense than mercury,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.960
that, in fact, you could fill the cup right to the top.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.860
So Neil obligingly tried it even though he was meant to be going on holiday

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.680
and it works.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.840
You can fill the Pythagorus Cup right to the top if you put a bit of mercury at the bottom

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.640
So what it happening is that the weight of the water

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.060
is just not enough to force the mercury up inside the siphon.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.760
If you fill it completely with mercury, you have enough weight pushing down

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.680
to make the siphon work.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.060
So the upshot is

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.740
that if Pythagoras' students had been clever,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.060
they could've put some mercury in the bottom of the cup

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.760
and filled it right to the top.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.280
The downside of course,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.200
is that they would have poisoned themselves.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.860
There were quite nice crystals that you could see formed

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.320
in the surface of the metal.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.000
And there was this great lump of mercury at the bottom, solid.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.460
But Neil had quite a lot of difficulty getting it out at the end

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.400
and it began to melt and so on.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.660
But that wasn't part of the experiment.

